WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:01.513
- I used to collect Pokemon cards,

00:00:01.513 --> 00:00:03.297
until one day I went to an Asian wedding

00:00:03.297 --> 00:00:04.663
and some kid stole it all

00:00:04.663 --> 00:00:06.400
and that's when I became an adult.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.732
(techno music)

00:00:09.732 --> 00:00:11.116
- I had Pokemon cards as a kid,

00:00:11.116 --> 00:00:12.573
but I just liked them for the pictures,

00:00:12.573 --> 00:00:13.481
I never played the game.

00:00:13.481 --> 00:00:15.901
- I spent so much time collecting them,

00:00:15.901 --> 00:00:18.739
and just to one day have them be gone,

00:00:18.739 --> 00:00:19.625
I was horrified.

00:00:19.625 --> 00:00:21.348
- It was kind of like Beanie
Babies where everyone was like,

00:00:21.348 --> 00:00:22.868
"One day these are gonna
be worth a bunch of money."

00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:25.733
And so, in my mind, the little
entrepreneur in me was like,

00:00:25.733 --> 00:00:28.650
keep these cards and then I didn't.

00:00:29.877 --> 00:00:30.710
- Oh it does, for sure.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:31.998
- One time, my brother and I bought

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:34.125
Yo-Gi-Oh! cards off of
eBay so I think that

00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:36.877
that can maybe inform my expertise.

00:00:36.877 --> 00:00:39.324
- I'm generally clueless
about the value of anything.

00:00:39.324 --> 00:00:42.468
I couldn't tell a $10
shirt from a $150 shirt.

00:00:42.468 --> 00:00:44.430
- My name's Chris, I'm with Core TCG,

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:47.351
we're a retail card gaming
shop in Pasadena, California.

00:00:47.351 --> 00:00:50.052
We do a ton of different
collectible card games

00:00:50.052 --> 00:00:52.876
and have fine expertise
in valuing specific cards.

00:00:52.876 --> 00:00:55.826
We've had experience with
Pokemon since the existence

00:00:55.826 --> 00:00:58.074
of the card game in the U.S. since 1999.

00:00:58.074 --> 00:00:59.224
With any collectible card games,

00:00:59.224 --> 00:01:01.708
value comes from supply and demand.

00:01:01.708 --> 00:01:03.676
How many cards of that
specific card are available

00:01:03.676 --> 00:01:04.509
on the market?

00:01:04.509 --> 00:01:05.957
Cards can be demanded by either

00:01:05.957 --> 00:01:07.973
collectors or players of the game.

00:01:07.973 --> 00:01:10.556
(bright music)

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.519
So they look pretty different,
these three Mewtwos.

00:01:13.519 --> 00:01:15.050
They're not like, three of the same card.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.159
- Right away, I recognized this one.

00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:18.337
I feel like I had one of these,

00:01:18.337 --> 00:01:19.944
and the fact that I had it,

00:01:19.944 --> 00:01:21.895
probably tells me that
it's not that expensive.

00:01:21.895 --> 00:01:24.913
- I am a Pokemon collector,
I definitely have these two,

00:01:24.913 --> 00:01:27.121
I've never seen this one
and it looks really fancy.

00:01:27.121 --> 00:01:28.785
- These I don't recognize at all,

00:01:28.785 --> 00:01:30.175
but they're shinier than the other one

00:01:30.175 --> 00:01:32.375
and usually the shinier
ones are more expensive.

00:01:32.375 --> 00:01:34.103
- So I think this cheapest Mewtwo,

00:01:34.103 --> 00:01:35.754
I think this is like a 50-cent card,

00:01:35.754 --> 00:01:37.621
a $10 card, $100.

00:01:37.621 --> 00:01:39.869
- $10, $250, $5,000.

00:01:39.869 --> 00:01:42.730
- Uh, uh, uh, uh, try
to look at the stats.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:43.563
- This is hard!

00:01:43.563 --> 00:01:44.980
- $10, $30, $100.

00:01:46.385 --> 00:01:50.034
- $250, $300, and I think this one's $500.

00:01:50.034 --> 00:01:52.841
- I would never spend
$30 on a card, like why?

00:01:52.841 --> 00:01:53.952
From a demand perspective,

00:01:53.952 --> 00:01:56.833
if a specific card is used
widely in tournament play,

00:01:56.833 --> 00:01:59.106
that can create a lot of
demand for that specific card.

00:01:59.106 --> 00:02:00.458
- $30 for a card?

00:02:00.458 --> 00:02:03.514
I mean, I guessed $5,000
which means I'm an idiot.

00:02:03.514 --> 00:02:04.492
- What?

00:02:04.492 --> 00:02:05.869
Do you people not like Pokemon,

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:07.195
what's wrong with you people?

00:02:07.195 --> 00:02:08.830
- $2, I've been tricked.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:09.915
- I'm feeling pretty confident,

00:02:09.915 --> 00:02:11.571
I started out on the right page,

00:02:11.571 --> 00:02:12.467
I got Mewtwo right.

00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:14.395
- I was very wrong, I got one right.

00:02:14.395 --> 00:02:16.845
- I got one right.
- I got one right.

00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:18.570
- I mean, how expensive can
Pokemon cards really get?

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.721
(emotional music)

00:02:20.721 --> 00:02:22.810
Two of them look almost exactly the same.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:23.953
- Damn, this is tricky

00:02:23.953 --> 00:02:25.360
- I have this Pikachu though,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.394
it's like before they made Pikachu skinny.

00:02:27.394 --> 00:02:28.977
- [Jordan] don't
recognize this one at all,

00:02:28.977 --> 00:02:30.658
it doesn't even look like Pikachu.

00:02:30.658 --> 00:02:32.988
- It tells me that it's PokeTour 1999,

00:02:32.988 --> 00:02:34.138
which means it's vintage,

00:02:34.138 --> 00:02:36.073
which means this one is
probably also vintage,

00:02:36.073 --> 00:02:37.625
but maybe not as vintage as this one.

00:02:37.625 --> 00:02:40.174
I feel like I'm in Mean Girls
because I keep saying vintage.

00:02:40.174 --> 00:02:41.007
- Based on the last one,

00:02:41.007 --> 00:02:43.616
the 2017 one was valued way higher,

00:02:43.616 --> 00:02:45.697
so I'm gonna say this
one's the most expensive,

00:02:45.697 --> 00:02:46.986
and then this will be the middle,

00:02:46.986 --> 00:02:49.203
and this will be the least expensive.

00:02:49.203 --> 00:02:50.637
- 10-cent card you can get in a pack,

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:51.484
$10 or so,

00:02:51.484 --> 00:02:53.412
I think this is like $100.

00:02:53.412 --> 00:02:54.662
- $5, $15, $45.

00:02:56.891 --> 00:02:58.224
- $10, $15, $40.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:00.467
- $2, $3, $30.

00:03:02.308 --> 00:03:03.355
- This one's 50 cents.

00:03:03.355 --> 00:03:04.385
I was very wrong.

00:03:04.385 --> 00:03:05.589
Extremely wrong.

00:03:05.589 --> 00:03:08.425
- $50, I was close, I guessed $30.

00:03:08.425 --> 00:03:09.626
- I got all three right in this group.

00:03:09.626 --> 00:03:11.706
- So I'm correct twice now,

00:03:11.706 --> 00:03:13.505
I'm feeling pretty good about myself.

00:03:13.505 --> 00:03:15.682
- I know everything there is
to know about Pokemon cards.

00:03:15.682 --> 00:03:16.834
- This one was $5,

00:03:16.834 --> 00:03:18.714
that's crazy that they're
the same card essentially,

00:03:18.714 --> 00:03:19.979
and this little piece of gold

00:03:19.979 --> 00:03:22.193
made this like a $45 difference.

00:03:22.193 --> 00:03:24.241
- When they released this game in 1999,

00:03:24.241 --> 00:03:26.106
they went around promoting this card,

00:03:26.106 --> 00:03:28.962
so this is a promotional item
that came out of Australia.

00:03:28.962 --> 00:03:29.969
And that's why it has that stamp

00:03:29.969 --> 00:03:32.273
for the Australian PokeTour
and it goes for $50.

00:03:32.273 --> 00:03:33.106
- I love Charizard,

00:03:33.106 --> 00:03:34.927
if you liked anything
other than Charizard,

00:03:34.927 --> 00:03:36.817
is because you're a contrarian like me.

00:03:36.817 --> 00:03:38.690
- I did Charizard, of course.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:40.069
I think Charizard's pretty lame,

00:03:40.069 --> 00:03:42.114
but I know these are
gonna be expensive because

00:03:42.114 --> 00:03:44.628
I feel like every little
boy loved Charizard.

00:03:44.628 --> 00:03:46.170
I started with Bulbasaur.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:47.547
- Now I'm looking for the PokeTour.

00:03:47.547 --> 00:03:49.669
Is there a date on it, is this a trick?

00:03:49.669 --> 00:03:51.131
These two look exactly the same.

00:03:51.131 --> 00:03:52.667
- I'm gonna say this is the most expensive

00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:55.515
and I guess these two
are the lesser expensive,

00:03:55.515 --> 00:03:56.653
but I'm definitely missing something

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:58.021
'cause I don't think
they're the same price.

00:03:58.021 --> 00:04:00.188
- [Jordan] Um, um, um, oh!

00:04:01.037 --> 00:04:02.275
I see, Edition 1.

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:03.984
- This means it's probably from like,

00:04:03.984 --> 00:04:05.784
the first pack ever.

00:04:05.784 --> 00:04:08.574
- That's probably really expensive then,

00:04:08.574 --> 00:04:09.407
maybe.

00:04:09.407 --> 00:04:11.204
- I'm just gonna say
baseline, they're all $50,

00:04:11.204 --> 00:04:13.789
because they're holographic
Charizard cards.

00:04:13.789 --> 00:04:16.093
So I'm gonna say $100 cheapest.

00:04:16.093 --> 00:04:18.188
$200 second-cheapest,

00:04:18.188 --> 00:04:20.923
and then $500 for the first
edition Charizard card.

00:04:20.923 --> 00:04:22.395
- This one's gonna be
like a straight-up guess:

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:24.062
$15, $30 card, $200.

00:04:25.057 --> 00:04:26.807
- [Kane] $1, $5, $50.

00:04:28.953 --> 00:04:31.336
- [Sydnee] $8, I think this one's $50,

00:04:31.336 --> 00:04:32.694
and I think this one's $100,

00:04:32.694 --> 00:04:34.488
because that's what I
value it in my heart.

00:04:34.488 --> 00:04:38.274
- So starting at $20 is
the cheapest Charizard,

00:04:38.274 --> 00:04:40.585
the most expensive Mewtwo
isn't that much more than that.

00:04:40.585 --> 00:04:43.544
- Whoa, holy crap, I was way wrong.

00:04:43.544 --> 00:04:45.289
This is $250?

00:04:45.289 --> 00:04:47.752
- I definitely have one
of these, this is crazy!

00:04:47.752 --> 00:04:48.992
Oh, this one's $20.

00:04:48.992 --> 00:04:53.073
- $250 is the middle ground,
how expensive does it get?

00:04:53.073 --> 00:04:54.281
(emotional music)

00:04:54.281 --> 00:04:55.114
Oh my God.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.251
- Oh my gosh, this thing's $2,000.

00:04:59.251 --> 00:05:00.427
- Okay, I'm gonna put that down,

00:05:00.427 --> 00:05:02.117
that makes me even scared to hold it.

00:05:02.117 --> 00:05:04.629
- I'm gonna take this
with me now (laughing)

00:05:04.629 --> 00:05:06.172
- This is a 1999 Charizard,

00:05:06.172 --> 00:05:09.748
you could get a used 1999
Toyota Camry for the same price.

00:05:09.748 --> 00:05:11.493
- You guys, I could get rich quick.

00:05:11.493 --> 00:05:12.747
- I got the price wrong,

00:05:12.747 --> 00:05:14.106
but I knew it was the most expensive,

00:05:14.106 --> 00:05:15.930
so I mean, half a point?

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:17.336
- The highest graded card we've have

00:05:17.336 --> 00:05:21.169
is a graded 9 Shadowless
First Edition Charizard,

00:05:21.169 --> 00:05:22.912
valued at about $7,000 or $8,000.

00:05:22.912 --> 00:05:25.312
- That is a shocking spread
of prices of Charizards,

00:05:25.312 --> 00:05:27.101
I cannot believe they got that expensive.

00:05:27.101 --> 00:05:29.068
And they look the same, this is insane.

00:05:29.068 --> 00:05:30.958
- I feel scared for
whoever owns this because

00:05:30.958 --> 00:05:33.454
it's made of paper and it could get wet.

00:05:33.454 --> 00:05:35.493
- I think it's cool, it brings
me back to my childhood,

00:05:35.493 --> 00:05:36.654
I think Pokemon's still cool,

00:05:36.654 --> 00:05:37.893
I don't think it's ever gonna stop

00:05:37.893 --> 00:05:40.005
'cause there's probably
like 800 of them now,

00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:40.838
probably more.

00:05:40.838 --> 00:05:41.800
- How do you stop someone from

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:43.296
just stamping "Edition 1" on a card

00:05:43.296 --> 00:05:44.975
and then telling you it's worth $2,000?

00:05:44.975 --> 00:05:48.121
- If I start today, by the time
my kids have Pokemon cards,

00:05:48.121 --> 00:05:49.367
they'll be billionaires.

00:05:49.367 --> 00:05:50.926
So, gotta think ahead.

00:05:50.926 --> 00:05:53.509
(upbeat music)

